                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/485: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Neville</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-29T17:00:00+09:00">November 29, 1929—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 29—6:35 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>112. My 111, November 27, 8 p.m. Minister for Foreign Affairs remains of the same
                    opinion as that previously expressed. The Foreign Office has been informed by
                    the Japanese Consul General at Mukden that Chang has been authorized by Nanking
                    to open negotiations with Soviets over the Chinese Eastern Railway question and
                    is ready to accede to the Russian demand for restoration of the Russian
                    directors on the railway as this would remove the original cause of the present
                    difficulty. The Japanese feel that nothing could be accomplished by outside
                    pressure.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Neville</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
